  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nMorning observations show a strong, 597+ dm ridge centered over\nthe southern CONUS. This ridge is forecast to remain at both a\nsimilar strength and position through the next several days,\nleading to near-record temperatures and dangerous heat indices.\nFor this afternoon, temperatures are forecast to peak in the\nmiddle to upper 90s. Surface dewpoints in the upper 60s and low\n70s will help yield heat index values in excess of 105 F for most\nof the region, particularly across eastern AR, West TN, and the\nMO Bootheel. Therefore, a Heat Advisory is in place through this\nevening at 8 PM CDT for the majority of the Mid-South.\n\nTo our north, thunderstorms over the Midwest have produced a\nfairly strong cold pool which has been observed moving south\nacross MO/IL this morning via surface observations and satellite\nimagery. Current model guidance shows this area of cooler air\nmerging with a weaker synoptic cold front through tonight,\neventually making it to the Mid-South early Thursday morning.\nEnough moisture may exist ahead of the boundary overnight to\nproduce a few showers or thunderstorms into Thursday around\nsunrise. However, the bulk of any precipitation is expected to\nexist in the form of isolated to scattered thunderstorms through\nthe afternoon hours across eastern AR, the MO Bootheel, and West\nTN. As of right now, the SPC has these areas outlined in a\nMarginal (level 1/5) Risk for severe weather, primarily for\ndamaging winds. Temperatures ahead of the front are expected to\nreach similar levels to today, although over a smaller area. A\nHeat Advisory has been placed near and west of the MS River\nThursday between 11 AM and 8 PM CDT. Some adjustments may be\nneeded to the Heat Advisory depending on the speed at which the\nfront moves through the region.\n\nMedium range guidance suggests that mid-level troughing\nassociated with the frontal passage may persist through Friday\nand into Saturday before the synoptic ridging begins to build\nagain. Afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances will therefore\nremain elevated with PoPs as high as 60% Friday and Saturday.\nHowever, forecast soundings indicate that a relative lack in\nsurface moisture content may impede the degree to which the\nregion will destabilize both days with only 1000 - 2000 J/kg\nMLCAPE. Weaker CAPE values would lead to weaker updrafts and a\nless pronounced severe wind threat, especially in the presence of\nweak bulk shear (< 20 knots). Additionally, the presence of\nwidespread rainfall and the passage of a weak front will cool\nmuch of the area into the low 90s and perhaps even\n\nEnsembles are in good agreement that the residual troughing\nFriday and Saturday will quickly become obsolete as the upper\nridge amplifies once again Sunday and into Monday. In turn, high\ntemperatures will climb back into the middle to upper 90s by\nSunday which are expected to persist through the end of the\nperiod. Lastly, while isolated afternoon convection is not out of\nthe question, the prevailing pattern over the region will be\nhostile to widespread rainfall with a drier forecast anticipated\ninto early next week.",
